# Give the wheel group read/write on the Goodix 55b4 sensor.
#
# Without a rule the node is crw-rw-r-- root:root, so libusb - which needs
# write access - fails for anyone but root, and every capture has to go through
# pkexec. That is one authentication prompt per operation, which makes the tool
# genuinely unpleasant to use during a debugging session.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" is the tidier mechanism and is what this should use: it hands
# the device to whoever is logged in at the seat and takes it back at logout.
# It did not work here - the rule matched (CURRENT_TAGS=:uaccess: confirmed via
# udevadm test), the session was local and active on seat0, and a real
# unbind/bind add event was generated, but systemd-logind never applied the
# ACL. Rather than ship a rule that quietly does nothing, this grants the group
# instead.
#
# What this means, stated plainly: any process running as a wheel member can
# then drive the fingerprint sensor directly - read frames, and on this driver
# also write the PSK. On a single-user laptop that is the same person who could
# already do it through pkexec; on a shared machine it is not. Prefer uaccess
# if it works on your system, and skip this file entirely if you only use the
# CLI or MCP modes under pkexec.
#
# Install:
#   sudo install -Dm644 99-fpstudio-goodix.rules \
#        /etc/udev/rules.d/99-fpstudio-goodix.rules
#   sudo udevadm control --reload
#   sudo udevadm trigger --attr-match=idVendor=27c6
#
# Remove: delete the file, reload, and replug or reboot.

SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="27c6", ATTR{idProduct}=="55b4", MODE="0660", GROUP="wheel"
